P20021.abstract:
In the extreme quantum limit, when the Landau level filling factor $\ensuremath{
u}<1$, the dominant electron-electron interaction in low-disorder two-dimensional electron systems leads to exotic many-body phases. The ground states at even-denominator $\ensuremath{
u}=1/2$ and $1/4$ are typically Fermi seas of composite fermions carrying two and four flux quanta, surrounded by the Jain fractional quantum Hall states (FQHSs) at odd-denominator fillings $\ensuremath{
u}=p/(2p\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1)$ and $\ensuremath{
u}=p/(4p\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1)$, where $p$ is an integer. For $\ensuremath{
u}<1/5$, an insulating behavior, which is generally believed to signal the formation of a pinned Wigner crystal, is seen. Our experiments on ultra-high-quality, dilute, GaAs two-dimensional electron systems reveal developing FQHSs at $\ensuremath{
u}=p/(6p\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1)$ and $\ensuremath{
u}=p/(8p\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1)$, manifested by magnetoresistance minima superimposed on the insulating background. In stark contrast to $\ensuremath{
u}=1/2$ and $1/4$, however, we observe a pronounced, sharp minimum in magnetoresistance at $\ensuremath{
u}=1/6$ and a somewhat weaker minimum at $\ensuremath{
u}=1/8$, suggesting developing FQHSs, likely stabilized by the pairing of composite fermions that carry six and eight flux quanta. Our results signal the unexpected entry, in ultra-high-quality samples, of FQHSs at even-denominator fillings $1/6$ and $1/8$, which are likely to harbor non-Abelian anyon excitations.

P20025.abstract:
Termination of the fractional quantum Hall states (FQHSs) and the emergence of Wigner crystal phases at very small Landau-level filling factors ($\ensuremath{
u}$) have been of continued interest for decades. Recently, in ultra-high-quality, dilute GaAs two-dimensional electron systems (2DESs), strong evidence was reported for FQHSs at $\ensuremath{
u}=1/7,2/13$, and 2/11, which fall in the $\ensuremath{
u}=p/(6p\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1)$ Jain series of FQHSs, interpreted as integer ($p\phantom{ule{0.16em}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{0.16em}{0ex}}1$, 2) QHSs of six-flux composite fermions ($^{6}\mathrm{CFs}$). These states are surrounded by strongly insulating phases which are generally believed to be Wigner crystals. Here, we study an ultra-high-quality 2DES confined to an AlAs quantum well where the 2D electrons have a much larger effective mass (${m}^{*}\ensuremath{\simeq}0.45{m}_{e}$) and a smaller dielectric constant ($\ensuremath{\epsilon}\ensuremath{\simeq}10{\ensuremath{\epsilon}}_{0}$) compared to GaAs 2D electrons (${m}^{*}\ensuremath{\simeq}0.067{m}_{e}$ and $\ensuremath{\epsilon}\ensuremath{\simeq}13{\ensuremath{\epsilon}}_{0}$). This combination of ${m}^{*}$ and $\ensuremath{\epsilon}$ renders the Landau-level mixing parameter $\ensuremath{\kappa}$, defined as the ratio of the Coulomb and cyclotron energies, $\ensuremath{\simeq}9$ times larger in AlAs 2DESs ($\ensuremath{\kappa}\ensuremath{\propto}{m}^{*}/\ensuremath{\epsilon}$). Qualitatively similar to the GaAs 2DESs, we observe an insulating behavior reentrant around a strong $\ensuremath{
u}=1/5$ FQHS, and extending to $\ensuremath{
u}<1/5$. Additionally, we observe a clear minimum in magnetoresistance at $\ensuremath{
u}=2/11$, and an inflection point at $\ensuremath{
u}=1/7$ which is very reminiscent of the first report of an emerging FQHS at $\ensuremath{
u}=1/7$ in GaAs 2DESs. The data provide evidence for developing QHSs of $^{6}\mathrm{CFs}$ at very small fillings. This is very surprising because $\ensuremath{\kappa}$ near $\ensuremath{
u}\ensuremath{\simeq}1/6$ in our sample is very large ($\ensuremath{\simeq}4$), and larger $\ensuremath{\kappa}$ has the tendency to favor Wigner crystal states over FQHSs at small fillings. Our data should inspire calculations that accurately incorporate the role of Landau-level mixing in competing many-body phases of $^{6}\mathrm{CFs}$ at extremely small fillings near $\ensuremath{
u}=1/6$.
